STEP 6: Deploy and install WordPress
------------------------------------
k apply -f .
k get pods -w           # mysql-0 Running, then wordpress Running
k logs sts/mysql | tail -3
k logs deploy/wordpress | tail -3

Add "127.0.0.1 wp.local" to /etc/hosts on the machine running kind
(or use port-forward: k port-forward svc/wordpress 8080:80).
Open http://wp.local in a browser (or via SSH tunnel), complete the
installer, write a blog post, and upload an image.

From the CLI:
  curl -s -H "Host: wp.local" localhost | grep -o "<title>.*</title>"


STEP 7: Prove persistence
-------------------------
k delete pod mysql-0
k delete pod -l app=wordpress
k get pods -w
Reload the site: the post and the image are still there.

Check the database directly:
  k exec -it mysql-0 -- mysql -uwpuser -pWpPass123 wordpress -e "select post_title from wp_posts where post_status='publish';"


STEP 8: Backup CronJob
----------------------
Write backup.yaml yourself using Day 27 Step 10 as a model:
  - PVC "mysql-backups" 1Gi
  - CronJob "mysql-backup", schedule "0 2 * * *", concurrencyPolicy
    Forbid, image mysql:8.4, command:
      mysqldump -h mysql-0.mysql -uroot -p"$MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD" wordpress > /backups/wp-$(date +%F-%H%M).sql
Test it now:
  k create job backup-now --from=cronjob/mysql-backup
  k logs job/backup-now
  (check the file size is not 0)


STEP 9: Disaster drill
----------------------
1) Note the post titles.
2) k delete ns wp            (everything gone, including PVCs!)
3) Recreate: k create ns wp; recreate the Secret; k apply -f .
4) The site is empty. That is why backups must live OUTSIDE the
   namespace/cluster (Week 7: cloud object storage).
